The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (len < num_chunks)
    return -EINVAL;
if (copy_to_user(to, ch->chunks, num_chunks))
    return -EFAULT;
Reason:
The length check only verifies that `len` (the user-provided buffer size) is at least `num_chunks` bytes, but it neglects the `sizeof(struct sctp_authchunks)` header (8 bytes) that precedes the chunks field in the user buffer. As a result, when `len` equals exactly `num_chunks` (e.g., for any `num_chunks > 0`), the check passes, yet `copy_to_user()` writes `num_chunks` bytes starting at offset `offsetof(struct sctp_authchunks, gauth_chunks) == 8`. This causes an out-of-bounds write of up to 8 bytes past the end of the allocated user buffer, overwriting adjacent userspace data.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (len < sizeof(struct sctp_authchunks) + num_chunks)
    return -EINVAL;
Reason:
The corrected length check now includes the `sizeof(struct sctp_authchunks)` header (8 bytes) plus the `num_chunks` bytes of chunk data. This ensures the user-provided buffer is large enough to hold both the header and the chunk list, preventing the out-of-bounds write. The `copy_to_user()` remains correct because it writes only `num_chunks` bytes starting at the `gauth_chunks` offset, which is now guaranteed to be within the buffer.

